# Introduction

This task explores the application of bootstrapping techniques to construct and analyze statistical properties such as confidence intervals, mean estimates, and the effect of outliers on sampling outcomes. Bootstrapping is a resampling method that uses repeated sampling with replacement from the observed data to estimate the variability of a statistic. The first part of the task involves performing bootstrap analyses on a small dataset of 12 values, computing measures such as the mean and median, and visualizing bootstrap distributions. The second part examines the impact of outliers by combining normally distributed data with a set of outliers, using bootstrapping to estimate various statistical measures and comparing the results graphically and numerically. Finally, we summarize the methodology of bootstrapping for constructing confidence intervals and conducting parametric and non-parametric tests.

This hands-on task provides a comprehensive exploration of bootstrap sampling's power, illustrating how it can be applied in different scenarios to derive robust statistical insights, even in the presence of outliers or small sample sizes.

