#### 1.3.4 Visualise the distribution all the different bootstrap means to the sample mean. Does the Central Limit Theorem kick in?

In this task, we will visualize the distribution of the bootstrap means and compare it to the sample mean of the original data. The goal is to observe whether the Central Limit Theorem (CLT) applies. According to the CLT, as we repeatedly sample from a population (with replacement) and calculate the mean of each sample, the distribution of these sample means should approximate a normal distribution, regardless of the original data's distribution. By plotting the bootstrap means and comparing them to the original sample mean, we can visually assess whether the CLT is at play and whether the distribution of the bootstrap means becomes more normal as the number of samples increases.

``` {r task1c}
tryCatch({
  hist(bootstrap_means, breaks = 30, main = "Distribution of Bootstrap Means", xlab = "Means", col = "skyblue")
  abline(v = mean_original, col = "red", lwd = 2)
}, error = function(e) {
  message("Error in plotting the histogram: ", e$message)
})
```
The Central Limit Theorem states that When you take repeated samples (with replacement) from a population and compute the mean of each sample, the distribution of these sample means will approach a normal distribution as the number of samples increases, even if the original data is not normally distributed. With a large enough number of bootstrap samples (e.g., 2000 as in your case), you should see that the distribution of bootstrap means becomes more symmetric and bell-shaped, which is an indication that the CLT is at play. The closer the shape of the bootstrap means is to the normal distribution, the more the CLT is visible.

