The results show the 95% confidence intervals (CIs) for the median based on three different bootstrap sample sizes (20, 200, and 2000) and a normality-based CI from a t-test. With only 20 bootstrap samples, the CI is relatively wide (4.765771 to 4.988062), indicating higher uncertainty and less precision. As the sample size increases to 200 bootstrap samples, the CI narrows (4.676396 to 4.975437), reflecting improved precision in the estimate of the median. With 2000 bootstrap samples, the CI becomes even more precise (4.687479 to 4.975854), providing a stable and accurate estimate. The normality-based CI from the t-test (4.674344 to 5.007323) is similar in width to the bootstrap intervals, suggesting that the median estimate under the normality assumption aligns well with the bootstrap method for this dataset. These results highlight how increasing the number of bootstrap samples enhances the reliability of the confidence interval, while the normality-based CI provides a comparable estimate under the assumption of normality.

