# Task 2 - Effect of outliers on Bootstrap Sampling

In this task, we will explore the impact of outliers on various statistical estimators using both nonparametric and parametric bootstrap methods. First, we will create two datasets: x.clean, which contains 1960 points sampled from a standard normal distribution, and x.cont, which contains 40 points sampled from a uniform distribution (4, 5). We will then combine these to form a data vector x. After setting the seed to your immatriculation number, we will estimate the median, mean, and trimmed mean (with alpha = 0.05) for both x and x.clean. Using nonparametric bootstrap sampling, we will calculate the standard error and 95% percentile confidence intervals for each estimator. Additionally, we will perform parametric bootstrap to compute bias, standard error, 95% percentile CI, and bias-corrected estimates for the mean and trimmed mean. For robustness, the scale of the data will be estimated using the median absolute deviation (MAD). Finally, we will summarize and compare the results using both tables and graphical visualizations.

#### 2.1 Set your seed to 1234. And then sample 1960 points from a standard normal distribution to create the vector x.clean then sample 40 observations from uniform(4,5) and denote them as x.cont. The total data is x <- c(x.clean,x.cont). After creating the sample set your seed to your immatriculation number.

``` {r task2a}
set.seed(1234)
x_clean <- rnorm(1960)
x_cont <- runif(40, min = 4, max = 5)
x <- c(x_clean, x_cont)
set.seed(12427550)
```

