# Task 3 - Bootstrapping for conifdence intervals and parametric or non-parametric tests

Bootstrapping is a resampling technique used to estimate the distribution of a statistic by generating additional samples from the observed data. It provides a powerful, flexible method for statistical inference, particularly useful when theoretical distributions are complex or unknown. Bootstrapping begins by taking an original dataset of size 
𝑛
n and creating a new sample of the same size by drawing observations randomly from the original data, with replacement. As a result, some data points may appear more than once, while others might be omitted in each resampled set.

Once a bootstrap sample is generated, the statistic of interest (such as the mean, median, or trimmed mean) is computed. This process is repeated many times, often thousands, producing a distribution of the statistic across all resamples. This distribution, known as the bootstrap distribution, provides insights into the variability and sampling behavior of the statistic. By examining the distribution, confidence intervals can be constructed, typically by identifying the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles for a 95% confidence interval. This approach is non-parametric because it relies solely on resampling from the data without assuming a specific parametric form of the population distribution.

There is also a parametric variant of bootstrapping, which differs from the non-parametric version in that it assumes the data follows a known distribution (such as normal). Instead of resampling directly from the data, parametric bootstrapping involves generating new samples from this assumed distribution with parameters estimated from the data (e.g., mean and variance). The resampled statistics are then analyzed similarly to the non-parametric approach to derive properties like bias, standard error, confidence intervals, and corrected estimates.

Bootstrapping serves as a basis for constructing confidence intervals through two main approaches: percentile-based and bias-corrected intervals. In the percentile-based method, the lower and upper bounds of the confidence interval correspond to percentiles from the bootstrap distribution. The bias-corrected approach adjusts for skewness or systematic biases observed in the bootstrap distribution to provide more accurate estimates. Non-parametric tests benefit from bootstrapping by allowing hypothesis testing without relying on traditional distributional assumptions, thus increasing robustness.

Overall, bootstrapping leverages data-driven resampling to construct empirical distributions for statistical inference. It is particularly useful for small samples, highly skewed data, or situations where theoretical distributions are inapplicable or difficult to derive.